Why Methods and Attributes?
    In this lesson, you’ve learned some subtle but powerful concepts that help you write cleaner code.

    With methods and attributes, each instance of a particular type contains its own set of values (attributes) and its own set of behaviors (methods) that you can use.

    These concepts help break down the complexity of a large program by bundling related information and functionality together in a single, self-contained package called an object (an instance).

    Functions vs. Methods
        Taking the str type as an example, imagine if you had to write a function outside of the type that checks if a string starts with a certain prefix:

            def check_if_starts_with(full_string, prefix_string):
                # logic here...
                pass

    This kind of function is called a top-level function because it exists on its own and isn't attached to a specific type. So far, most of the functions you’ve written have been top-level functions.

    However, there are significant advantages to using methods and attributes over top-level functions:

        Readability: Putting the capabilities of a type together with the type itself makes the code easier to follow. 
        my_string.startswith("A") reads almost like a sentence.

        Autocompletion: When you type a dot (.) after a variable, modern code editors only show you the methods that work for that specific type. 
        If every function were a top-level function, typing a single letter would show you every possible function in your entire program!

        Organization: 
            Documentation is much easier to navigate. Instead of searching a massive list of global functions, you can look up the "String" category to see everything a string can do.

    Hiding Complexity
        Earlier, you learned that a function can hide a lot of complex logic while still being easy to use. 
        Instances are just an expansion of that same idea.

        You know that every string instance can tell you if it starts with a certain letter or if it is all uppercase. 
        The logic behind how Python actually scans those characters is hidden from you. 
        You don't need to know the details of how the str type works under the hood; you just call the methods and attributes to get the job done.